WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.790
Between Guardians of the Galaxy, Avengers:
Infinity War, and Avengers: Endgame, just

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:08.100
about everyone on the planet has gotten a
look at Josh Brolin as Thanos.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.269
But how they'd pull the transformation off?

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:14.440
Let's take a deep dive into how the MCU turned
Josh Brolin into the Mad Titan.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:19.700
According to the visual effects supervisor
Kelly Port, whose company Digital Domain worked

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.880
on creating Thanos for Infinity War, the number
of people it takes to complete a major studio

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.300
movie's visual effects has exploded in recent
decades.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.580
Marvel's own Iron Man 3 has over 1,800 people
credited in its visual effects department

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:37.080
alone — a dramatic step up from the special
effects-heavy blockbusters of decades past.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.100
Port said to The Verge,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.809
"We were reflecting back on Titanic days,
the late '90s, where a big effects film would

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.960
be considered around 300 shots.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.860
Whereas [Infinity War], in contrast, has over
3,000 visual effects shots."

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.080
"And now, it's here.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.940
Or should I say, 'I am.'"

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.020
Speaking in an interview with Art of VFX,
Marvels Studios' overall VFX supervisor Dan

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.740
gave some insight into the amount of work
that goes into a movie's visual effects before

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.040
anyone's even powered on their editing rig.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.460
Speaking of Avengers: Infinity War, DeLeeuw
said,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.620
"This movie wasn't grounded like Winter Soldier
or Civil War.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.190
It involved eight to ten foot tall aliens
and battles on other planets.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:21.080
We knew the movie was going to be massive
and had to plan accordingly."

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.240
The company's plan, according to DeLeeuw,
involved

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:28.290
"[dividing up] the movie into individual sequences
that would be awarded to separate VFX houses.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.040
Each house would get a sequence that would
tell a complete narrative.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.840
This way each house could maintain the look
and feel of the sequence while minimizing

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.110
the need for shared shots."

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:42.020
The responsibility of introducing Thanos to
the world in Infinity War was shouldered by

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.650
Digital Domain, which took on a workload of
513 shots amounting to over 40 minutes of

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:47.180
screen time.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.380
Their work involved bringing Thanos to life
in every scene except the one on Titan, a

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.700
sequence which was instead handled by WETA
Digital.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.980
While you might not have heard of the studios
by name before, you're certainly familiar

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:58.900
with their work.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.320
WETA is the studio behind The Lord of the
Rings' Gollum and the new Planet of the Apes

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.370
movies' Caesar, while Digital Domain been
working in movies since James Cameron's True

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:07.780
Lies in 1994.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.220
Founded by Cameron and acclaimed special effects
artist Stan Winston, Digital Domain has been

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.640
working on large-scale superhero movies since
X-Men in 2000, and have since had a hand in

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.040
making the first Thor, the third Iron Man,
Spider-Man: Homecoming, and Thor: Ragnarok

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.300
with Marvel Studios.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.520
Even with the team's extensive experience,
it took over 340 people from the company nearly

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.980
two years to bring just most of Thanos' parts
to fruition, which really puts things into

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.680
perspective when it comes to how much pure
labor it takes to make movies of this scale

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:36.220
work.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.680
Directors Anthony and Joe Russo met with Brolin
and the digital effects artists several months

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.560
before live shooting began, to both see what
Marvel wanted to accomplish with the character,

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:49.989
and also to determine what the effects companies
would be capable of turning in.

00:02:49.989 --> 00:02:53.599
Digital Domain's Kelly Port said it was a
proud moment to show off Digital Domain's

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:57.530
first Thanos test to Brolin and the directors
on the actor's first day of shooting.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:58.530
Port said,

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.159
"They were all very excited about it, and
I think there was a collective sigh of relief

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:03.340
that we could pull this off."

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.129
The results of Digital Domain's first test
were encouraging, showing that the company's

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:11.409
artists were capable of maintaining Brolin's
humanity as they encased his performance in

00:03:11.409 --> 00:03:12.440
a virtual skin.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.200
"It's just the way it is.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:15.780
I'm just a messenger."

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.440
According to Dan DeLeeuw,

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.060
"The early test [showed] how much we could
do with the character.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:24.420
It showed that would could take exactly what
Brolin did on stage and reproduce it with

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:25.340
the CG character."

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:30.660
The test put Brolin at ease, letting the actor
know he didn't need to oversell his performance

00:03:30.669 --> 00:03:32.140
to make the character feel alive.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.310
As Kelly Port put it,

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:37.349
"I think what was really great about his reaction
was his sense that he did not, as an actor,

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:38.400
need to overplay the acting."

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:42.240
Seeing that the effects team would be able
to effectively translate a lower-key Thanos

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:46.409
performance in all of its subtleties was a
boon to the filmmakers — especially Brolin,

00:03:46.409 --> 00:03:49.420
who was worried about how he would come off
in a motion-capture performance.

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:53.940
No one was more nervous about bringing Thanos
to life than Josh Brolin.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.639
Playing the Mad Titan wasn't as physically
demanding as his live-action performance as

00:03:57.639 --> 00:04:00.359
Cable in Deadpool 2, but it was emotionally
draining.

00:04:00.359 --> 00:04:01.860
As Brolin described it,

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.569
"I was more nervous doing that than I've been
for a long time."

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:07.249
While shooting the movie, Brolin at times
found himself having a hard time striking

00:04:07.249 --> 00:04:10.940
an imposing presence while wearing an unflattering
motion capture suit.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:11.940
Brolin said,

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.569
"You've got to walk around in a onesie and
imagine yourself as an actor with some impact.

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:19.460
[...] I'm sitting there and I'm looking like
I look, which is not 700 pounds, and purple,

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.360
and eight feet tall."

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:25.520
To record Brolin's performance, the production
installed motion-capture cameras all around

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.139
the movies' sets, allowing other actors more
space to interact with him.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.520
The movements of his body were recorded by
those cameras, while close-up shots were recorded

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.420
by HD cameras that were attached to a helmet
and pointed back at his face.

00:04:37.009 --> 00:04:41.040
All of the footage was then put into a massive
database, with every tiny movement of Brolin's

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:45.229
face being used for reference when it came
time to replace his features with the computer-generated

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:46.240
Thanos model.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.120
The goal, according to Kelly Port, was to
have the CG model match the emotions of the

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.180
real-life actor.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:52.180
Port said,

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.949
"We look at the result; you look at Brolin
side by side with Thanos.

00:04:54.949 --> 00:04:57.200
Is he conveying the same emotional expression?

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.319
Is he conveying the same emotion with his
face?

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:02.090
And we make a subjective call."

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:06.159
One of the more interesting hurdles to overcome
in bringing Thanos to life wasn't making sure

00:05:06.159 --> 00:05:10.919
that he looked like Josh Brolin, but rather
making sure that he looked like… well, himself.

00:05:10.919 --> 00:05:15.060
Fans of the MCU have been anticipating Thanos'
arrival ever since the end of The Avengers

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.620
in 2012, when the big purple space monster
first turned to the camera with a sinister

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:20.620
grin.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.470
This was years before Brolin was ever cast
or considered for the part in Infinity War,

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:29.090
so how did the pre-existing Thanos from prior
movies fit into the character's final form?

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.630
According to Port, the Digital Domain team
started their Thanos' modeling with the old

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:35.270
look, and slowly incorporated Brolin's features.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:38.980
There was no magic to the process — just
a long back and forth between their teams

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:40.139
and the people at Marvel.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:40.640
Port said.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:45.120
"We tried to find that balance, but we did
introduce more of Brolin I think than in previous

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.000
Thanoses in the films."

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:49.500
The effects teams' efforts weren't immediately
met with enthusiasm.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.350
Upon the release of the first footage of Brolin
as Thanos, many fans criticized the new look,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:57.240
finding that too much had changed between
the initial and final versions.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:02.780
The most difficult aspect of bringing Thanos
to life had to do with his quiet, reflective

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:03.280
moments.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.980
While an effects-heavy battle scene has lots
of moving parts from a VFX perspective, it's

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:11.500
also easier to cut corners when the screen
is so busy with meteors, explosions, and Spider-Man

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:12.000
antics.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.840
In other words, it's not that hard to make
the purple space man fight the big green guy.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.940
It's much harder to make you believe the purple
space man can cry.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.680
Speaking with Art of VFX, Kelly Port said
the most complicated Thanos scene is his sacrifice

00:06:23.689 --> 00:06:25.290
of Gamora on Vormir.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:25.920
Port said,

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:30.060
"Many of the shots of Thanos in this sequence
are very close and very emotional, so it was

00:06:30.069 --> 00:06:33.370
extremely important that Brolin's performance
come through for this scene."

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:38.070
The importance of nailing Thanos' ability
to physically convey emotion can't be overstated.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.630
One false move, and the filmmakers fall directly
into the uncanny valley.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:45.050
Once you remember you're looking at something
made in a computer, it's easy to be taken

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:46.099
out of the story.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:49.240
You'd basically be watching Jar-Jar Binks
cry — and there's nothing engrossing about

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:49.740
that.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:50.780
"How rude."

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:55.830
While the idea of getting into such a pivotal
mo-cap role was daunting, Brolin has since

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:59.310
gone on to call Thanos, quote, "one of the
most favorite jobs I've ever had."

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:03.120
Part of getting into the character involved
immersing himself in nearly 50 years' worth

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.380
of Marvel Comics history.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:08.280
Of course, Marvel Studios didn't just send
Brolin to the local comic book shop with a

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:09.260
gift card.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:09.920
According to Brolin,

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.580
"They sent me over this bible, just all this
information.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.620
It was a massive thing, and yet even that
was maybe a sixteenth of what there was to

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:17.120
learn."

00:07:17.629 --> 00:07:21.500
As anyone who's ever read comic books is well
aware, the internal consistency of the characters

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:26.139
and their stories can sometimes be a nightmare,
especially as characters mutate across decades

00:07:26.139 --> 00:07:27.319
under different writers.

00:07:27.319 --> 00:07:31.069
"The comic book and Marvel world is not only
extremely emotional to people, but so in-depth.

00:07:31.069 --> 00:07:33.010
There's no way to learn it all.

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:36.080
But you try to do what you can [...] I started
reading and I was in awe."

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:40.700
The cliche goes that eyes are windows to the
soul, which is really just a way of saying

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.250
they convey subtlety.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:47.649
They squint or widen to show fear, shock,
or focus, conveying emotion through tiny microexpressions

00:07:47.649 --> 00:07:51.719
— and when it comes to a monstrously evil
figure like Thanos, seeing those emotions

00:07:51.719 --> 00:07:53.370
can make all the difference.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.520
According to Anthony Russo, who co-directed
Infinity War and Endgame,

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:00.430
"Even though he's despicable on so many levels,
there's a part of Thanos that is very empathetic.

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:03.699
He has a very complex inner life, and he's
not all bad."

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:04.220
The filmmaker also called Josh Brolin,

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:09.100
"...a performer who's capable of delivering
that kind of complexity, where you have that

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:13.000
level of violence in him, but at the same
time you have that level of sensitivity."

00:08:13.009 --> 00:08:14.280
All of Brolin's acting work would be for nothing
if Digital Domain proved incapable of mapping

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:20.620
Brolin's face onto a monster — but fortunately,
the studio was up to the task.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.200
According to Port,

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:26.520
"What we really focus on is the eyes at Digital
Domain, and there's a ton of work, and a huge

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.120
amount of detail around that."

00:08:28.449 --> 00:08:33.100
The company doesn't treat the eye as one object,
but rather as layers of intersecting objects,

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:35.870
making the final product feel more complex
and alive.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:39.780
All of this goes a long way toward making
Thanos feel like a real person — and that

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:41.660
realism, in turn, makes him so much scarier.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:46.980
Another one of the tricky parts of animating
a CGI character has to do with making the

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:50.790
audience believe the character actually exists
on the same plane as real actors.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.230
The most important element to focus on in
this aspect is lighting.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:55.230
According to Port,

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:57.709
"If the lighting is not good, it won't look
photoreal."

00:08:57.709 --> 00:08:58.220
A CGI character starts off being lit by nothing.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.200
When the character is placed into real-world
footage that is saturated with a certain kind

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:07.860
of lighting, a lack of similar lighting on
the CGI model will look disastrous.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:10.380
Speaking to Art of VFX, Port said,

00:09:10.389 --> 00:09:14.720
"As we began to experiment with lighting ideas,
we soon discovered that Thanos looked his

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.120
best with slightly more complex lighting with
various colors rather than monochromatic light."

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.740
Many of the scenes Digital Domain was tasked
with took place in darker settings, on spaceships

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:28.360
or stormy planets, giving the team some flexibility
when it came to lighting Thanos' skin.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:31.820
For the scene when Thanos arrives on Earth
in broad daylight, they had to contend with

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:35.480
making him look like he was being lit by the
same sunshine as the performers.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:36.480
Port said,

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.170
"[We] found that having Thanos walk through
the dappled light of the trees was very effective

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.750
for integrating him into the shot and giving
him depth."

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:45.260
The years of work paid off with Infinity War's
debut.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:48.540
During an interview conducted after the movie's
release, Port said,

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:52.020
"The excitement of the audience's reaction
seeing the film for the first time was really,

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.020
really cool for me.

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:57.300
We do it for the love of the craft, but we
also do it for the love of entertaining.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:59.690
The reception from the audience is so rewarding.

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:00.240
It means a lot."

